narrative:
## Controlling How Data is Laid Out

XIOM gives you control over memory layout for performance optimization and FFI compatibility.

### Default Layout

The compiler may reorder fields for optimal size:

```xiom
pub type Record = {
  name: Str;    // heap pointer
  id: Int;      // 4-8 bytes
  active: Bool; // 1 byte
}
// Compiler may reorder: id, active, name for compactness
```

### C Layout with #[repr(C)]

```xiom
#[repr(C)]
pub type CRecord = {
  id: Int;
  active: Bool;
  name: *const Char;  // C string pointer
}
// Fields stay in declaration order — C-compatible
```

### Packed Layout

```xiom
#[repr(packed)]
pub type PackedRecord = {
  a: Bool;  // 1 byte
  b: Int;   // 4 bytes — no padding, may be misaligned!
  c: Bool;  // 1 byte
}
// Total: 6 bytes (no padding) — but b may be misaligned
```

Use `packed` sparingly — misaligned access is slow and can cause faults on some platforms.

### Layout Transitions

```xiom
// Default: compiler-optimized layout
pub type Internal = { x: Int; y: Int; }

// FFI: fixed C layout
#[repr(C)]
pub type External = { x: Int; y: Int; }

// Protocol: packed layout
#[repr(packed)]
pub type Protocol = { header: Int; payload: [100]Int; }
```

### When to Care About Layout

- FFI: always use `#[repr(C)]`
- Binary protocols: may need packed
- Performance optimization: default layout is usually best
- Cache efficiency: group frequently-accessed fields together

